	affiliations = {Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Macao, 999078, China; School of Economics and Business Administration, Chongqing University, Chongqing, 400030, China},
	abstract = {Using 23,357 firm-year observations of Chinese A-share listed firms from 2010 to 2023, this paper examines how local fiscal pressure (LFP) causally suppresses corporate social responsibility (CSR). The core identification challenge is endogeneity: OLS estimates conflate fiscal stress with broader economic decline. We therefore combine fixed-effects models, two-stage least squares with a within-province fiscal deviation instrument, and a continuous difference-in-differences design exploiting China's 2016 VAT reform—three strategies jointly suited to isolating the causal effect of LFP from confounding regional trends. LFP significantly reduces CSR through three channels: reduced government subsidies, tightened financing constraints, and eroded investment confidence. Effects are stronger for non-state-owned firms, regions with weak environmental regulation, and less marketized areas. These findings imply that fiscal sustainability is a structural determinant of corporate social behavior, with practical relevance for intergovernmental transfer design and location-adjusted ESG benchmarking. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Inc.},
